\title{A level that, when reached, closes the position and opens the same size the other way}
\description{
This is the Atlas's G13, which turns a long of 75 into a short of 75 or the other way. Like a \code{CloseOnTriggerOrder}, it first cancels every order resting on the instrument to free margin, acts on the net position held when it fires, and completes without an order when nothing is held. With \code{method} set to \code{sequential}, UBI sends a closing order and, once it has filled completely, a second order of the same size and side that opens the reverse; with \code{double}, it sends one order for twice the position, which is faster but needs the broker to accept margin for the new side before the old one closes. Both are limits two ticks past the other side's best price. Set \code{transaction_type} to the side that opened the position. \code{trigger_price} here is the level, not the order's own trigger. With \code{trigger_on}, the level is compared with the bid, the offer or the midpoint instead of the last trade, or must be reached on two ticks in a row (\code{double_last}) or for \code{hold_seconds} (\code{held}), so a single stray trade does not fire it. It answers HTTP 202 with an \code{outcome} of \code{armed} and sends nothing to a broker until it fires, so keep the \code{parent_id} from the answer. With \code{sequential}, the reverse is sent on the side the close traded and waits until every broker's close is done, then opens the whole reverse at the broker of the first close, sized to what the close filled.

The order template's attributes are described on \code{SyntheticOrder}.
}
